{"name":"White Christmas","hex":"#f4e8e8","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"white","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Bright and colourless; reflecting equal quantities of all frequencies of visible light.","Of or relating to Europeans or those of European descent, regardless if their skin has cool or warm undertones."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The color of snow or milk; the color of light containing equal amounts of all visible wavelengths.","A person of European descent with light-colored skin."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/white"}],"entries":[{"title":"White Christmas (weather)","blocks":[{"text":"A white Christmas is a Christmas with the presence of snow, either on Christmas Eve or on Christmas Day, depending on local tradition. The phenomenon is most common in the northern countries of the Northern Hemisphere. This is because December is at the beginning of the Southern Hemisphere summer, and so white Christmases there are extremely rare—with the exception being Antarctica, the Southern Alps of New Zealand's South Island, and parts of the Andes in South America."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d8/Christmas_card_from_the_Duke_and_Duchess_of_Windsor_%2846318774022%29.jpg/330px-Christmas_card_from_the_Duke_and_Duchess_of_Windsor_%2846318774022%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/White_Christmas_(weather)","wikidata":"Q615160"},{"title":"White Christmas (song)","blocks":[{"text":"\"White Christmas\" is a song reminiscing about an old-fashioned Christmas setting. Written by Irving Berlin for the 1942 musical film Holiday Inn, the song won the Academy Award for Best Original Song at the 15th Academy Awards."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a7/Bing_Crosby_-_White_Christmas_1942_10_inch.jpg/330px-Bing_Crosby_-_White_Christmas_1942_10_inch.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/White_Christmas_(song)","wikidata":"Q1131761"},{"title":"Christmas","blocks":[{"text":"Christmas is a festival commemorating the birth of Jesus Christ, observed primarily on December 25 as a religious and cultural celebration among billions of people around the world. A liturgical feast central to Christianity, Christmas preparation begins on the First Sunday of Advent. Advent is followed by Christmastide, which historically in the West lasts twelve days and culminates on Twelfth Night. Christmas Day is a public holiday in many countries and is observed by a majority of Christians; it is also celebrated culturally by many non-Christians and forms an integral part of the annual festive holiday season."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8f/NativityChristmasLights2.jpg/330px-NativityChristmasLights2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Christmas","wikidata":"Q19809"},{"title":"White","blocks":[{"text":"White is the lightest color and is achromatic. It is the color of objects such as snow, chalk, and milk, and is the opposite of black. White objects fully reflect and scatter all the visible wavelengths of light. White on television and computer screens is created by a mixture of red, blue, and green light. The color white can be given with white pigments, especially titanium dioxide."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e2/Delphinapterus_leucas_2.jpg/330px-Delphinapterus_leucas_2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/White","wikidata":"Q23444"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/01/White_Christmas_trailer_%281954%29_title_frame.jpg/500px-White_Christmas_trailer_%281954%29_title_frame.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/01/White_Christmas_trailer_%281954%29_title_frame.jpg/500px-White_Christmas_trailer_%281954%29_title_frame.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Title screenshot from the theatrical trailer for the film White Christmas (1954)","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AWhite_Christmas_trailer_(1954)_title_frame.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/02/Probability_of_a_white_Christmas_in_the_United_States_1981-2010.jpg/500px-Probability_of_a_white_Christmas_in_the_United_States_1981-2010.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/02/Probability_of_a_white_Christmas_in_the_United_States_1981-2010.jpg/500px-Probability_of_a_white_Christmas_in_the_United_States_1981-2010.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Probability of a white Christmas in the contiguous United States from 1981 to 2010","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AProbability_of_a_white_Christmas_in_the_United_States_1981-2010.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/75/A_white_Christmas_-_geograph.org.uk_-_2207761.jpg/500px-A_white_Christmas_-_geograph.org.uk_-_2207761.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/75/A_white_Christmas_-_geograph.org.uk_-_2207761.jpg/500px-A_white_Christmas_-_geograph.org.uk_-_2207761.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Christmas snow in Oxfordshire, England","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AA_white_Christmas_-_geograph.org.uk_-_2207761.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/17/Snow_in_the_Christmassy_streets_of_Trondheim_2.jpg/500px-Snow_in_the_Christmassy_streets